{"name":"Uniform Grey","hex":"#a8a8a8","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"uniform","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Uniform of the ICAO/NATO radiotelephony alphabet."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unvarying; all the same.","Consistent; conforming to one standard."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/uniform"},{"term":"grey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray.","Synonym of coloured (pertaining to the mixed race of black and white)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey"}],"entries":[{"title":"Uniform","blocks":[{"text":"A uniform is a variety of costume worn by members of an organization while usually participating in that organization's activity. Modern uniforms are most often worn by armed forces and paramilitary organizations such as police, emergency services, security guards, in some workplaces and schools, and by inmates in prisons. In some countries, some other officials also wear uniforms in their duties; such is the case of the Commissioned Corps of the United States Public Health Service or the French prefects. For some organizations, such as police, it may be illegal for non-members to wear the uniform."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/The_Royal_Marines_from_1664_to_1896.jpg/330px-The_Royal_Marines_from_1664_to_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uniform"},{"title":"Grey","blocks":[{"text":"Grey or gray is an intermediate color between black and white. It is a neutral or achromatic color, meaning that it has no chroma. It is the color of a rain or storm cloud, of ash, and of lead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg/330px-Loxodonta_africana_